{
  "topic": "Should schools allow generative AI for homework?",
  "essay": "Generative AI is becoming part of education, and schools need a balanced approach to homework. It can explain difficult ideas and help students begin assignments, but students also need to think for themselves. A complete ban seems unrealistic because technology is available outside school. Unrestricted use would be unfair as well. Schools should allow AI in some circumstances while maintaining clear expectations.\n\nOne advantage is that students can ask questions without feeling embarrassed. A learner who does not understand a science explanation can request a simpler version. AI may also suggest an essay outline or help a student notice a grammatical problem. These uses can save time and make learning more interesting. However, a clear explanation may still be wrong, so students should check it against reliable classroom materials. Teachers remain important because they understand the course and know their students.\n\nThere are also concerns about cheating. Some students might submit a complete answer without understanding it. This means homework would no longer show what they can do. Schools could ask students to describe how they used AI and to provide examples of their own thinking. Teachers could sometimes ask follow-up questions in class. This would encourage responsibility, although it would also require additional work from teachers.\n\nFairness is another issue. Students do not all have the same devices or subscriptions, and some families can offer more guidance than others. Schools should provide equal access where possible. They should also teach digital literacy so that everyone understands the benefits and risks. Technology is changing rapidly, and education needs to keep up with these changes.\n\nIn conclusion, AI can be a useful homework assistant if students use it honestly and carefully. Schools should create sensible rules, support teachers, and help students become independent learners. A balanced approach can protect academic integrity while preparing students for a future in which technology will play an important role.",
